t 27 18:44 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   20941 Oct 27 18:44 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent      16 Oct 27 18:22 results -> ../investigation\ndrwx------  2 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:51 revisions\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:51 scientific-evidence\ndrwx------  3 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:22 source_evidence\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     252 Oct 27 18:29 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     258 Oct 27 18:29 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       7 Oct 27 18:22 task -> ../task\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    8986 Oct 27 18:51 validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    7851 Oct 27 18:51 validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   19809 Oct 27 18:51 validation_report.json\n# Paper validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-10-27T18:51:36.819121+00:00\n- paper_dir: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- agentic evidence audit failed: see /home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/paper/paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json and /home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md\n- figure validation failed: figures[1] figure_type does not match strategy for per_family_reliability\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `5178`\n- substantive_element_count: `6`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `96`\n- resolved_references: `19`\n- anchored_references: `77`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `6`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `4`\n- summary: Nearly all numbers, CIs, roster counts and figure encodings reproduce from paper_data.json and the analysis files, and the downstream selection scope is disclosed correctly, but the SI misstates which checks produced the qualified outcome, one mechanism is stated as established fact, one headline difference is misrounded, and the unscored variant is described in more detail than the evidence supports.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `si-qualification-gates-1` (Supplementary Information / Evaluation protocol and validity diagnostics): The paper presents a specific list of gates as the basis for outcome: qualified. The evidence only grounds the outcome values, and the evaluator's gate logic differs from the list. capped_walks is a reported diagnostic and not part of the correctness condition. The actual condition also needs a nonconstant predictor, positive predictions, a non-vacuous permutation, no baseline failure and an exhausted RPC budget, none of which are listed. How the harness maps correct or feasible to qualified is not visible, so the cause of the outcome is partly unknown.\n  - claim: The checks behind this outcome are: zero isomorphic overlap ... zero walks reaching the step cap; zero failed episodes; and passing closed-form simulator checks on C12, K10 and P10.\n  - evidence: paper_data.json grounding_sources final.qualification.outcome/status/correct carry only the values qualified/completed/true; final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified; analysis/evaluator_snapshot/evaluate_final.py lines 1253-1256 (plane_valid = sim_ok and baseline_failure is None and overlap == 0 and complete and failed == 0; episode_valid includes positive, order_diff <= ORDER_TOL, not perm_vacuous, nonconstant; correct also needs rpc_count == rpc_budget) and line 1246 (capped_walks is only recorded as a metric); claims_grounding.json claim 'The sealed held-out evaluation completed with outcome qualified' cites only final.qualification.*\n  - requested_fix: State only that the sealed evaluation completed with outcome qualified (correct=true). Present overlap, order-invariance, capped walks, failed episodes and simulator checks as reported diagnostics, not as the checks behind the outcome. If gates are described, cite the evaluator's correctness condition accurately, drop capped walks from it, and say the mapping to the qualified label is not reported.\n- `results-matthews-causal-2` (Results / Main result): This comparison has point estimates only, with no paired interval. The paper still states a definite win and gives a causal explanation as fact. No experiment isolates that cause, and the Limitations section says no component attribution was done and that significance language is avoided for point-estimate comparisons.\n  - claim: Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale ...\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics has matthews_ref_log_err=0.6053 and no delta_vs_matthews CI; claims_grounding.json Matthews claim says 'point estimates only' and makes no causal claim; paper.tex Limitations 'Per-family uncertainty and small families' and 'No component attribution' paragraphs; paper_data.json ablation_studies=[] and evidence_sources.ablations.status=missing\n  - requested_fix: Change it to say the predictor has a lower point-estimate error (0.183 vs 0.605) than the Matthews-type reference, with no paired interval. Present the explanation as an untested hypothesis, e.g. 'one possible explanation is ...'.\n- `results-delta-rounding-3` (Results / Main result and SI Table tab:family): The point difference behind this value rounds to -1.215 at the three decimals reported, not -1.214. Every other three-decimal value in the paper is rounded correctly, so this one does not match the evidence trail.\n  - claim: The paired difference is -1.214 log units (also the Family-weighted mean row of Table tab:family: -1.214)\n  - evidence: analysis/derived_metrics.json best_bound_minus_predictor_plane=1.2145704200093006; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics best_bound_log_err=1.3973218 and final_main_log_err=0.1827514 (difference 1.21457); claims_grounding.json claim 2 repeats -1.214\n  - requested_fix: Change -1.214 to -1.215 in the Results paragraph and the Family-weighted mean row of Table tab:family, or report it as -1.21.\n- `si-unscored-variant-detail-4` (Supplementary Information / Selection scope): The only artifact record of the unscored variant is its title. There is no source evidence for it, so implementation details such as 'positive mixture' and 'cross-validated residual' are not supported. They also conflict with the same section's statement that no claims are made about the variant. The attempt counts, the retained baseline, the 0.193 development-pool score and the separate-scope caveat are correctly grounded.\n  - claim: One alternative model variant was designed before the sealed evaluation: a positive mixture of physically motivated scaling terms with a cross-validated residual.\n  - evidence: paper_data.json idea_titles=['Physics scaling mixture with CV log-L1 correction']; downstream_study.attempt_counts {total:1, scored:0, unscored:1}; source_evidence/ contains only the downstream baseline and program, which are identical starter ridge code; paper_data.json all_programs=[]\n  - requested_fix: Describe the variant only by its recorded title (e.g. 'one alternative model variant, titled a physics scaling mixture with a cross-validated log-L1 correction, produced no score'). Drop the implementation details, or say they are unverified.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.5 < 10.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1630 < 2400\n- figures[2].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.5 < 10.0\n- figures[2] PNG width too small: 1636 < 2400\n- figures[2] PNG height too small: 696 < 700\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n**Summary:** The draft (`paper.tex`) cites only five classical cover-time sources. Two citation gaps are worth fixing with a small, targeted retrieval pass:\n\n1. One comparator has no citation, and another is credited to the wrong paper.\n2. The positioning and discussion text refers to family-specific cover-time asymptotics without citing any.\n\nNeither fix needs a rewrite of the paper's story.\n\n## Current coverage\n\nThe draft's current coverage was checked against `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`:\n\n- **Aleliunas et al. 1979.** Cited for the 2m(n−1) bound. Adequate.\n- **Chandra et al. 1989.** Cited for the commute-time and effective-resistance link. Adequate.\n- **Lovász survey.** Cited for the closed forms and general background. Adequate.\n- **Feige 1995, \"A Tight *Lower* Bound on the Cover Time\".** Cited both for the n ln n lower bound and for the (4/27)n³ *upper* bound. The upper-bound attribution is wrong; see Target 1.\n- **Retrieval failures.** The original retrieval hit HTTP 429 on the queries for the 2m(n−1) bound and Feige's upper bound. The query for the Matthews bound returned no candidates. This explains the gaps below.\n\nAll quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and `analysis/` through `@evidence` tags. None of them needs a literature citation.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. `related-work-comparators`: Background and Related Work (also Introduction, Method §Compared predictors, Discussion). Priority **high**. Intent: comparator.\n\n**Anchors:**\n- \"Matthews' method bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$.\"\n- \"Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case…\"\n\n**Why this is high priority:**\n- **Matthews has no citation.** The Matthews-type reference is a row in the main results table (Table 2, error 0.605), and the text discusses it as \"a more demanding comparison\". It is never cited, either in Background or in the Compared predictors list.\n- **The cubic bound is credited to the wrong paper.** The best bound min{2m(n−1), (4/27)n³} is the primary comparator. Its cubic term is credited to Feige1995ATL, which is the lower-bound paper. The upper bound comes from Feige's separate tight-upper-bound paper. The same misattribution recurs in the Introduction, the Design rationale and the Discussion, where the lollipop attains (4/27)n³.\n- **Reviewers will check.** Reviewers at a venue that rewards honest baselines will check where each comparator comes from.\n\n**Search topics:**\n- Matthews, \"Covering problems for Markov chains\"\n- Feige, \"A tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\"\n- Optional textbook anchor for the Matthews method and hitting-time computation: Levin–Peres–Wilmer, *Markov Chains and Mixing Times*\n\n### 2. `positioning-family-asymptotics`: Background and Related Work (Positioning), supporting the Discussion. Priority **medium**. Intent: benchmark_context.\n\n**Anchor:** \"it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, or asymptotics for specific families\"\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- **The Positioning paragraph leaves a gap.** It points to family-specific asymptotic work but cites none of it.\n- **The Discussion depends on that work without citing it:**\n  - It says random 3-regular and dense Erdős–Rényi graphs lie in the near-n ln n regime, which is where the gains are largest.\n  - It suggests that grid cover times are not a single power law at this scale, which is offered as the reason grids have high error.\n- **One to three citations would close the gap.** Citations for random-regular, random-graph and 2D-torus cover-time asymptotics would ground both points and connect the chosen test families to known results. The narrative stays unchanged.\n\n**Search topics:**\n- Cooper–Frieze, cover time of random regular graphs\n- Cooper–Frieze, cover time of sparse random graphs (Erdős–Rényi)\n- Dembo–Peres–Rosen–Zeitouni, cover times for random walks in two dimensions (torus)\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n\n- **Quantitative results** (Tables 2–3, per-family numbers, controls). These are grounded in evaluator output.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261027_182218.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f01b5249-a810-4c3c-883d-d238d18952c4","uuid":"705539ab-1a38-470e-90e1-de51870effec","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:51:41.021Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 5840\ndrwx------  9 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:51 .\ndrwx------ 10 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:48 ..\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     695 Oct 27 18:22 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------  3 agent agent    4096 Oct 27 18:22 .claude\nprw-------  1 agent agent       0 Oct 27 18:51 .paper_repair_round_1_stream_20261027_182218.fifo\n-rw-------  1 agent agen