{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTFEEU4Vadv4wDgHYt9H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WzyxrkMNyneY7Y3LBN4n8X","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"F=/workspace/task/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/src/orchestrator/paper_step.py; sed -n 215,228p $F; grep -n \"fig:\\|includegraphics\\|1\\.214\\|Matthews\\|qualified\\|positive mixture\\|capped\\|beat a structure\\|The reason is\" paper.tex","description":"See aliases and locate target passages in paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1136,"cache_read_input_tokens":47772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1136,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f01b5249-a810-4c3c-883d-d238d18952c4","uuid":"cbd0330a-da06-46f1-b0b4-f51c7b0b4874","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:51:56.986Z","request_id":"req_011CgTFEE22BLfQwjf7TDD2M"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WzyxrkMNyneY7Y3LBN4n8X","type":"tool_result","content":"    \"composite\": 4,\n    \"diagram\": 1,\n}\nSTRATEGY_FIGURE_TYPE_ALIASES = {\n    \"flow_diagram\": \"diagram\",\n    \"horizontal_bar\": \"bar\",\n}\n\n\ndef _normalized_strategy_figure_type(value: object) -> str:\n    figure_type = str(value).strip()\n    return STRATEGY_FIGURE_TYPE_ALIASES.get(figure_type, figure_type)\n\n\n73:Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method, presented in the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. \\citet{Chandra1989TheER} showed that commute times equal $2m$ times the effective resistance between the endpoints. As a result, the cover time lies within a logarithmic factor of $m\\,R_{\\max}$, where $R_{\\max}$ is the largest effective resistance between any two vertices. Since $R_{\\max}$ is at most the diameter, the product $m\\cdot\\diam(G)$ is a natural cheap proxy for this resistance scale. Exact hitting times and effective resistances need a linear solve on the full adjacency matrix, so they are not ``cheap statistics'' in our sense. We still include a Matthews-type reference (maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$) as a graph-aware comparison point. The survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} collects the classical closed forms for paths, cycles, complete graphs and stars. These closed forms are used here only to check the simulator (\\S\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n84:\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth,height=0.3\\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/overview_concept.png}\n86:\\label{fig:overview}\n95:and a Monte Carlo estimate $\\hat \\Ccov(G)$ of its uniform-start cover time with standard error. After fitting, the predictor sees only $x(G)$ for each held-out graph. It sees no adjacency information, family label, graph identifier or held-out label, and it must return a strictly positive prediction $\\hat y(G)$. The error on one graph is the absolute log ratio, capped at $\\ln 1000$:\n118:\\item \\textbf{Matthews-type reference} (Matthews' bound; see \\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}): the exact maximum hitting time (from a linear solve on the full transition matrix) times $H_{n-1}$. It needs the whole graph, so it marks what full structural information gives a bound-based predictor.\n152:Walks are capped at $40\\cdot 2m(n-1)$ steps; no training or held-out walk reached the cap. The median relative standard error of the held-out labels is 0.033. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.capped_walks]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.mc_rel_se_median]]\n182:Matthews ref.\\ ($\\max H\\cdot H_{n-1}$) & full graph & 0.605 & 1.83 & --- \\\\\n196:\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/plane_level_comparison.pdf}\n197:\\caption{Held-out mean absolute log error by predictor. (a) Fitted predictors and the permuted-feature control; the error bar on our predictor is its 95\\% two-level bootstrap CI. (b) Bound-based predictors. The Matthews reference needs the full graph. The x-axis is shared across panels.}\n198:\\label{fig:plane}\n201:Table~\\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\\ref{fig:plane} summarize held-out accuracy. The six-statistic predictor has mean absolute log error 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), so a typical prediction is off by a factor of about 1.20 (CI about 1.18--1.23). The best of the two generic bounds has error 1.397, a typical factor of about 4.0. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#geo_ratio_predictor]] @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#geo_ratio_best_bound]]\n202:The paired difference is $-1.214$ log units, with 95\\% CI $[-1.303,-1.130]$. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#best_bound_minus_predictor_plane]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_ci_high]]\n208:A more demanding comparison is the Matthews-type reference, which uses exact hitting times from the full transition matrix. Its error is 0.605, better than either generic bound but still above that of the six-statistic predictor (point estimates; the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison). Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, which a bound must cover with a worst-case constant.\n225:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/per_family_reliability.pdf}\n227:\\label{fig:family}\n230:Figure~\\ref{fig:family} and Table~\\ref{tab:family} in the Supplementary Information address the second research question. The predictor's own error varies by a factor of almost seven across families. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#per_family_error_spread_ratio]]\n237:The paired gain over the best bound (Figure~\\ref{fig:family}b) has a 95\\% interval excluding zero in 9 of 10 families. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#n_families_ci_excludes_zero]]\n251:Sorting families by the best bound's error (Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a) gives an almost monotone pattern in the gains. The derived best-bound error is smallest on lollipops (0.275) and paths (0.476), the two families whose shapes are closest to the extremal constructions for the cubic and spanning-tree bounds. It is largest on complete (2.433) and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs (2.453). % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#per_family]]\n262:For a practitioner with a small graph from one of the evaluated families, the six-statistic predictor gives a point estimate typically within about 20\\% of the expected cover time. It is more reliable on tree-like, regular and star-like graphs than on dense, random or grid graphs. When a guaranteed budget is required, the generic bounds remain the right tool, and their slack can be read off Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a: for lollipop-like graphs the cheaper of the two bounds is already accurate to within a typical factor of about 1.3.\n274:\\paragraph{Per-family uncertainty and small families.} Per-family errors of the predictor are reported without intervals. Grids have only five held-out graphs and six training graphs. Per-family paired intervals exist for the gain over the best bound but not for the other comparators. The Matthews, constant and single-bound comparisons are point estimates without paired intervals, and we avoid significance language for them.\n316:Family-weighted mean & 116 & 0.183 & 1.397 & $-1.214$ & $[-1.303,\\ -1.130]$ \\\\\n326:The sealed held-out evaluation completed, and the predictor met the evaluation's qualification criteria (outcome: qualified). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n339:The evaluated predictor is the reference starter configuration supplied with the evaluation harness, used as is. One alternative model variant was designed before the sealed evaluation: a positive mixture of physically motivated scaling terms with a cross-validated residual. It produced no performance score. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f01b5249-a810-4c3c-883d-d238d18952c4","uuid":"fd23e6e2-840c-4a37-9105-9c81471b26af","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:51:57.051Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    \"composite\": 4,\n    \"diagram\": 1,\n}\nSTRATEGY_FIGURE_TYPE_ALIASES = {\n    \"flow_diagram\": \"diagram\",\n    \"horizontal_bar\": \"bar\",\n}\n\n\ndef _normalized_strategy_figure_type(value: object) -> str:\n    figure_type = str(value).strip()\n    return STRATEGY_FIGURE_TYPE_ALIASES.get(figure_type, figure_type)\n\n\n73:Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method, presented in the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. \\citet{Chandra1989TheER} showed that commute times equal $2m$ times the effective resistance between the endpoints. As a result, the cover time lies within a 