/plane_level_comparison.pdf}\n197:\\caption{Held-out mean absolute log error by predictor. (a) Fitted predictors and the permuted-feature control; the error bar on our predictor is its 95\\% two-level bootstrap CI. (b) Bound-based predictors. The Matthews reference needs the full graph. The x-axis is shared across panels.}\n198:\\label{fig:plane}\n201:Table~\\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\\ref{fig:plane} summarize held-out accuracy. The six-statistic predictor has mean absolute log error 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), so a typical prediction is off by a factor of about 1.20 (CI about 1.18--1.23). The best of the two generic bounds has error 1.397, a typical factor of about 4.0. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#geo_ratio_predictor]] @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#geo_ratio_best_bound]]\n202:The paired difference is $-1.214$ log units, with 95\\% CI $[-1.303,-1.130]$. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#best_bound_minus_predictor_plane]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.delta_vs_best_bound_ci_high]]\n208:A more demanding comparison is the Matthews-type reference, which uses exact hitting times from the full transition matrix. Its error is 0.605, better than either generic bound but still above that of the six-statistic predictor (point estimates; the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison). Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, which a bound must cover with a worst-case constant.\n225:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/per_family_reliability.pdf}\n227:\\label{fig:family}\n230:Figure~\\ref{fig:family} and Table~\\ref{tab:family} in the Supplementary Information address the second research question. The predictor's own error varies by a factor of almost seven across families. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#per_family_error_spread_ratio]]\n237:The paired gain over the best bound (Figure~\\ref{fig:family}b) has a 95\\% interval excluding zero in 9 of 10 families. % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#n_families_ci_excludes_zero]]\n251:Sorting families by the best bound's error (Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a) gives an almost monotone pattern in the gains. The derived best-bound error is smallest on lollipops (0.275) and paths (0.476), the two families whose shapes are closest to the extremal constructions for the cubic and spanning-tree bounds. It is largest on complete (2.433) and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs (2.453). % @evidence[[analysis/derived_metrics.json#per_family]]\n262:For a practitioner with a small graph from one of the evaluated families, the six-statistic predictor gives a point estimate typically within about 20\\% of the expected cover time. It is more reliable on tree-like, regular and star-like graphs than on dense, random or grid graphs. When a guaranteed budget is required, the generic bounds remain the right tool, and their slack can be read off Figure~\\ref{fig:family}a: for lollipop-like graphs the cheaper of the two bounds is already accurate to within a typical factor of about 1.3.\n274:\\paragraph{Per-family uncertainty and small families.} Per-family errors of the predictor are reported without intervals. Grids have only five held-out graphs and six training graphs. Per-family paired intervals exist for the gain over the best bound but not for the other comparators. The Matthews, constant and single-bound comparisons are point estimates without paired intervals, and we avoid significance language for them.\n316:Family-weighted mean & 116 & 0.183 & 1.397 & $-1.214$ & $[-1.303,\\ -1.130]$ \\\\\n326:The sealed held-out evaluation completed, and the predictor met the evaluation's qualification criteria (outcome: qualified). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n339:The evaluated predictor is the reference starter configuration 