{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTFEvzc4DgsVYmt1wXt1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WnY9wDUDcQYH96SRqpFtzw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"The paired difference is $-1.214$ log units\",\"The paired difference is $-1.215$ log units\"),\n(\"Family-weighted mean & 116 & 0.183 & 1.397 & $-1.214$\",\"Family-weighted mean & 116 & 0.183 & 1.397 & $-1.215$\"),\n(\"Its error is 0.605, better than either generic bound but still above that of the six-statistic predictor (point estimates; the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison). Global statistics plus fitted coefficients thus beat a structure-aware bound at this scale. The reason is that the fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it learns the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, which a bound must cover with a worst-case constant.\",\n \"Its error is 0.605, lower than either generic bound. The six-statistic predictor has a lower point-estimate error (0.183 vs.\\\\ 0.605), but the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison, so we do not attach a significance statement to it. One possible explanation, which we have not tested, is that a fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it can learn the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, whereas a bound must cover that ratio with a worst-case constant.\"),\n(\"The sealed held-out evaluation completed, and the predictor met the evaluation's qualification criteria (outcome: qualified). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\\nThe checks behind this outcome are: zero isomorphic overlap among the training, development and held-out pools; a maximum relative difference of 0 between predictions on the main and reordered queries; zero walks reaching the step cap; zero failed episodes; and passing closed-form simulator checks on $C_{12}$, $K_{10}$ and $P_{10}$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episodes]]\",\n \"The sealed held-out evaluation completed with outcome \\\\emph{qualified} (the evaluator's correctness flag is true). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\\nIn the evaluator source, the correctness flag requires passing closed-form simulator checks, no failure of the reference baseline, zero isomorphic overlap between pools, complete held-out coverage and zero failed episodes; in addition, every episode must return strictly positive, non-constant predictions that are invariant to query order and must pass a non-vacuous feature-permutation control, and the full query budget must be used (\\\\texttt{analysis/evaluator\\\\_snapshot/evaluate\\\\_final.py}, lines 1253--1256). How the harness maps this flag to the \\\\emph{qualified} label is not reported. Separately, the evaluator reports the following diagnostics: zero isomorphic overlap among the training, development and held-out pools; a maximum relative difference of 0 between predictions on the main and reordered queries; zero walks reaching the step cap (recorded as a diagnostic, not part of the correctness condition); and zero failed episodes. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.failed_episodes]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.capped_walks]]\"),\n(\"One alternative model variant was designed before the sealed evaluation: a positive mixture of physically motivated scaling terms with a cross-validated residual. It produced no performance score.\",\n \"One alternative model variant, recorded only by its title (``Physics scaling mixture with CV log-L1 correction''), was prepared before the sealed evaluation. It produced no performance score, and its implementation is not part of the inspectable evidence.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:60]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"1.214\\|beat\\|thus\\|reason is\" paper.tex claims_grounding.json; grep -n \"capped_walks\\|iso_overlap\" paper_data.json | head -4; grep -n \"alternative model variant\\|Matthews\" paper.tex | head","description":"Apply audit text fixes to paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":933,"cache_read_input_tokens":54816,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":933,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":41,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f01b5249-a810-4c3c-883d-d238d18952c4","uuid":"4485e03b-e339-42bd-85db-f7f09b80c141","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:52:15.787Z","request_id":"req_011CgTFEvbJSZEJBuuQWtNs7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WnY9wDUDcQYH96SRqpFtzw","type":"tool_result","content":"claims_grounding.json:13:      \"claim\": \"The best of 2m(n-1) and (4/27)n^3 has mean absolute log error 1.397, factor about 4.0; paired difference -1.214 with 95% CI [-1.303,-1.130]; predictor removes about 87% of the best bound's log error.\",\npaper.tex:252:A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is the shape that asymptotically attains $(4/27)n^3$ \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}. When a bound is nearly tight for a family, no predictor can beat it by much on that family. When a family lives near the opposite extreme, the bound must overshoot by a factor that grows with $n$, and any reasonable fitted model gains a lot. The latter case is exemplified by complete graphs, whose cover time $(n-1)H_{n-1}$ sits at the $n\\ln n$ lower-bound regime \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO,Feige1995ATL}. Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs show a similarly large bound slack in our measurements (Table~\\ref{tab:family}); we read this as consistent with a near-$n\\ln n$ regime for these families at $n\\le 30$, but we did not test that reading separately. Our predictor does not need to understand the extremal combinatorics. It only needs to place a graph on the right scale, and the six statistics are enough for that.\n85:      \"capped_walks\": 0.0,\n141:      \"iso_overlap_count\": 0.0,\n185:    \"final.metric.capped_walks\": {\n187:      \"id\": \"final.metric.capped_walks\",\n73:Tighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method, presented in the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. \\citet{Chandra1989TheER} showed that commute times equal $2m$ times the effective resistance between the endpoints. As a result, the cover time lies within a logarithmic factor of $m\\,R_{\\max}$, where $R_{\\max}$ is the largest effective resistance between any two vertices. Since $R_{\\max}$ is at most the diameter, the product $m\\cdot\\diam(G)$ is a natural cheap proxy for this resistance scale. Exact hitting times and effective resistances need a linear solve on the full adjacency matrix, so they are not ``cheap statistics'' in our sense. We still include a Matthews-type reference (maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$) as a graph-aware comparison point. The survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} collects the classical closed forms for paths, cycles, complete graphs and stars. These closed forms are used here only to check the simulator (\\S\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n118:\\item \\textbf{Matthews-type reference} (Matthews' bound; see \\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}): the exact maximum hitting time (from a linear solve on the full transition matrix) times $H_{n-1}$. It needs the whole graph, so it marks what full structural information gives a bound-based predictor.\n182:Matthews ref.\\ ($\\max H\\cdot H_{n-1}$) & full graph & 0.605 & 1.83 & --- \\\\\n197:\\caption{Held-out mean absolute log error by predictor. (a) Fitted predictors and the permuted-feature control; the error bar on our predictor is its 95\\% two-level bootstrap CI. (b) Bound-based predictors. The Matthews reference needs the full graph. The x-axis is shared across panels.}\n208:A more demanding comparison is the Matthews-type reference, which uses exact hitting times from the full transition matrix. Its error is 0.605, lower than either generic bound. The six-statistic predictor has a lower point-estimate error (0.183 vs.\\ 0.605), but the evaluator reports no paired interval for this comparison, so we do not attach a significance statement to it. One possible explanation, which we have not tested, is that a fitted model is not constrained to overestimate: it can learn the typical ratio between cover time and its scale, whereas a bound must cover that ratio with a worst-case constant.\n274:\\paragraph{Per-family uncertainty and small families.} Per-family errors of the predictor are reported without intervals. Grids have only five held-out graphs and six training graphs. Per-family paired intervals exist for the gain over the best bound but not for the other comparators. The Matthews, constant and single-bound comparisons are point estimates without paired intervals, and we avoid significance language for them.\n339:The evaluated predictor is the reference starter configuration supplied with the evaluation harness, used as is. One alternative model variant, recorded only by its title (``Physics scaling mixture with CV log-L1 correction''), was prepared before the sealed evaluation. It produced no performance score, and its implementation is not part of the inspectable evidence. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n345:This study was carried out with substantial use of large language models. An LLM-based research agent drafted the alternative model variant mentioned above, inspected the predictor and evaluator source, selected the framing, generated the figures from the recorded metrics, and wrote this manuscript, including all text, tables and captions. The evaluation harness (graph generation, Monte Carlo labelling, comparison predictors, bootstrap) and the evaluated predictor configuration were supplied with the task. Every number in this paper is taken from the recorded evaluation output or derived from it by the documented script. LLMs were not used to generate or alter experimental measurements.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f01b5249-a810-4c3c-883d-d238d18952c4","uuid":"931d80ba-b921-4df0-b0d6-a8ddbcc81732","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:52:15.910Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"claims_grounding.json:13:      \"claim\": \"The best of 2m(n-1) and (4/27)n^3 has mean absolute log error 1.397, factor about 4.0; paired difference -1.214 with 95% CI [-1.303,-1.130]; predictor removes about 87% of the best bound's log error.\",\npaper.tex:252:A lollipop with a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices is t